WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.930
Film Courage: You are here in Los Angeles
screening a film at the Pan African Film Festival?

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.930
Mark Harris, Filmmaker: Yes.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.320
Film Courage: And are you a full-time filmmaker
in Chicago or do you have another job?

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.053
Mark: I’m a full-time filmmaker in Chicago,
this is what I do.

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:15.810
This is how I provide for my family.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.850
I don’t do anything else but make films.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:26.290
Again, I have a marketing company (what I
use to market my films).

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.130
The way I provide for my family is through
filmmaking.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.820
Film Courage: For filmmakers who haven’t
figured out the business side yet what advice

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.440
can you give them?

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.250
And how can you explain the ways that a filmmaker
can get out of having a 9-to-5 job.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.250
Mark: Great question.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:49.899
Here’s how you can definitely get out.

00:00:49.899 --> 00:00:58.770
Say for instance you asked the question a
filmmaker that makes a film for $20,000.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:03.420
What you have to do is you have that $20,000.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.949
Within 2 months how can I make $40,000?

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.840
You want to do a certain amount of screeners
so you can bring in at least $40,000.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:21.420
Now that $20,000 if you use your own money
to make that film or you use investor’s

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:27.020
money then you know off the top that $20,000
is going to go back to paying the investors

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.020
back.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.359
So you have your investors and you pay your
investors back.

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:40.490
So you have a profit of another $20,000, what
you do with that $20,000 if you have a home

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:48.000
(your own place, an apartment), you use a
portion of that $20,000 to pay your rent off

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.279
for 6 months so you know for 6 months that
are you covered with your rent.

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:06.009
Say after you pay you have another $10,000
left over.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:11.599
What you do with that $10,000 is you have
more screenings.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:18.290
You may have another screening and invest
maybe $3,000 of that money in another screening.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:25.750
And don’t look to always want to screen
your film in LA (it’s very expensive).

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:31.290
Look at the Southern places, look at Charlotte,
look at Atlanta (Atlanta is pretty expensive

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.549
too), look at places like South Carolina,
North Carolina, Nashville.

00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:41.829
Go to different places like that where they
are looking for filmmakers to come in and

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:46.680
screen their films and they will support because
no one else is doing this.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:53.900
And so if you take $3,000 of that money and
you screen for maybe three days and you charge

00:02:53.900 --> 00:03:01.020
$10 a day at 300 seats that’s a total of
$3,000 a night.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.790
Now within 3 nights you are making $9,000.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:11.980
So that’s a total of $9,000 you made within
3 days depending on the number of seats that

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.609
you want to rent out.

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:19.340
Now you take that money and you make sure
you save.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.530
You already know that your rent is paid up
for 6 months, right?

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:28.109
Now you have this film and you are good for
6 months because your rent is paid up for

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:29.109
6 months.

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:30.420
That’s stress that is no longer there.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:37.900
Because a lot of things that keep us from
being free is the fear and the stress of not

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.780
being able to pay our rent.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.770
We know we have this 6 months.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.950
Now we know that we have this film that we
made for $20,000 right?

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:55.050
Now we want to look at what are we going to
do to get this film sold?

00:03:55.050 --> 00:04:00.819
We want to start pitching this film to Netflix
and Amazon and different companies who are

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:03.909
always looking for content.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:07.030
You start to pitch the film and while you
are pitching the film you still have time.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.379
You can still be screening it too.

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:15.680
As you are screening the film at these different
theaters you are constantly making money.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.900
You have to be very careful of the numbers.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:26.139
So a $20,000 film you could make $100,000
if it’s done properly within the entire

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:31.990
year and that’s profit if it’s done properly.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:42.370
But you have to hit these cities like Nashvilles,
like the Charlottes like the South Carolinas.

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.930
These cities that nobody is going to.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:45.930
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:48.550
So not LA, not New York, probably not Seattle?

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.060
Mark: I wouldn’t do New York, I wouldn’t
do LA.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.810
I would do Chicago.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.210
Here is why I would do Chicago.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:58.280
Chicago has a lot of theaters that are very
affordable.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:05.500
For instance, I can screen at the Ice Theater
(a theater in Chicago).

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.870
They may charge me $800 to screen this film.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.780
But you are looking at a theater that seats
300 people.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.560
Charge $12.00, sell it out, that’s a profit.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:23.810
You do that for three nights you just made
what?

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:24.900
You made $9,000.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:30.400
Film Courage: So how are you getting people
do go to these theaters if you are not from

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.400
that area?

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.569
Like if I wanted to go to Nashville and I
want to buy some time in a theater, then how

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:37.849
am I getting the word out?

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:38.849
Mark: Great question.

00:05:38.849 --> 00:05:41.550
So this is what I would normally do.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:49.560
If I screen my film, if I screen in Charlotte
(which we have done) I would partner with

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:56.610
someone, a film festival or someone who does
marketing for clubs and things like that (a

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:57.610
promoter).

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.479
Because you have all these promoters (thank
goodness for social media).

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:08.190
If you know someone who is a promoter in one
of these cities, send them an inbox and say

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:12.220
I have a film I want you to promote and maybe
we will do a 70/30 deal.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.379
I get 70, you’ll get 30.

00:06:15.379 --> 00:06:21.409
Now the promoter who maybe promotes for clubs
or parties and things like that, they have

00:06:21.409 --> 00:06:27.100
someone different that they can make money
off of.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.729
If it’s something that makes sense business
wise, they are open to it.

00:06:31.729 --> 00:06:35.930
They may take your film and say “I’ll
promote it for you in my city.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:37.030
I can get the people out.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:39.539
We’ll do a 70/30 split.

00:06:39.539 --> 00:06:43.050
I’ll take 70 as the filmmaker, you take
30.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.949
You’ve got to give them something for their
time.

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:53.370
They will get the people out to the theater
you just do that.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.800
Because there are promoters all over the country.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:02.759
In one city you can find thousands and thousands
of promoters, just link up with a promoter.

00:07:02.759 --> 00:07:06.800
Or link up with a film festival.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:13.009
Even like the smaller film festivals in those
different cities, most cities you have film

00:07:13.009 --> 00:07:14.009
festivals.

00:07:14.009 --> 00:07:17.509
Just link up with the director of the film
festival, someone who works with the film

00:07:17.509 --> 00:07:21.500
festival and just say I’m looking to screen
my film.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.139
Are you looking to make some extra money.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:31.110
And if you are, can we partner and you help
me promote it, come out and we do a 70/30

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.169
split.

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:34.700
Most times, who is going to turn that down?

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:41.750
If I have a built-in audience that I’m already
promoting to and I know I can get 300 people

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:49.550
out to a theater, if I screen a film there,
that’s money to be made.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:51.009
It’s a business deal.

00:07:51.009 --> 00:07:53.409
Film Courage: Have you tried the same thing
here in LA?

00:07:53.409 --> 00:07:57.360
I know you said you wouldn’t do it but I
am just curious if you’ve tried it?

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.070
Other than with film festivals.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:05.270
Mark: Like when I did BLACK COFFEE, we screened
the film in LA.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:10.699
But if was more for marketing, not to make
money.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:23.199
But outside of that Chicago, Charlotte is
a great place to screen your film.

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:26.250
They are looking for more independent films
to support.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:33.260
Film Courage: So when you did [screen] in
LA, you realized that it would probably be

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:37.400
a loss and it would just be to get the word
out and you were okay with this?

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:38.400
Mark: Yes.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:46.140
And here’s the thing, say for instance if
you just want to get the marking and just

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.360
to say “I screened my film in LA.”

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:55.750
To use that as a tool for the other cities,
you can do a screening in LA knowing that

00:08:55.750 --> 00:09:01.620
you may break even or you may make a profit
or you may not make a profit.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.200
You may loose, you may go in the red.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.890
But you can still say I screened my film in
LA.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:08.130
LA has a lot of power.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:13.440
Again when you say you’ve screened your
film in LA it’s going to put the antennas

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.960
up and people want to know “Okay, that’s
cool.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:16.960
In LA?”

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:22.380
So we can use that as leverage (even in New
York).

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.880
Chicago not so much but in LA, New York you
can use that as leverage for the smaller cities.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.170
Film Courage: Interesting, I did not know
that.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:35.300
How did you figure the business model out
in how to make money with these smaller films?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:42.520
Mark: I always knew that I had to keep the
films very low.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:50.520
So for me, $20,000 was too much to spend on
a film.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.320
Especially if you don’t have named actors
in it.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.950
So I knew I’m going to keep the films about
$10,000.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.570
And from $10,000, I knew I can make that back
within a weekend.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:11.070
So I knew that I’m want to keep these films
low, I’m going to make that profit back

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:14.130
and I’m going to reinvest and do more screenings.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:26.800
By keeping it so low, if you have investors,
you can pay your investors back whether it’s

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.650
$1,000, $5,00 whatever.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:37.180
Because a 90-minute film is 90-minute film,
right?

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:44.090
So people don’t know what you paid to get
the film made, they are not aware of that.

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:47.340
You don’t have to tell people “I made
this film for $20,000.”

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:55.540
No one needs to know that but if a film like
BLACK PANTHER comes out, of course you have

00:10:55.540 --> 00:11:00.430
millions and millions of dollars for marketing
and you have people who are going to rush.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.240
But it’s still a feature film.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.860
A $20,000 feature film is a feature film.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:17.790
So if you’re going to shoot that film for
$20,000 you have to look at it like it’s

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:19.830
a studio film, even though it’s not a studio
film.

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:23.420
But you have to treat it like “This is my
studio film.”

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.190
Because again, we are a studio, you are a
studio.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:33.660
We may not have the hundreds of millions of
dollars to do marketing to put in a film.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:38.980
But your $20,000 film, you have to use the
same method and process that the studios are

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.670
doing for this film.

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:49.061
So you take it out, screen it, you make money
off it it, you continue to screen it, because

00:11:49.061 --> 00:11:52.920
again, people want to see those independent
films.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.240
Like Hollywood?

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:55.260
The blockbuster stuff.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:12:00.750
So this is a perfect time for independent
filmmakers because everything that’s coming

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.070
out of Hollywood is blockbuster.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:10.240
And we want to see a good little story, we
want to see a good dramatic film, a good story

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:13.140
without things having to be blown up.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.710
So this is like the perfect time for us.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:24.360
You take it to those small cities and screen
your film.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:28.640
You can make money, you can make a living
off of just screening your film.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:31.560
Film Courage: Have all of your films made
money?

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.500
Mark: All of them, every last one of them.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:37.880
Every last thing I’ve ever made all made
a profit.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:43.020
Because if I’m making a film for $1,000
or $10,000, if I can’t make a profit of

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.410
it that, I don’t need to be making films.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:54.600
If I make the movie I made BLACK BUTTERFLY,
which I made for less than $10,000.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.600
We sold it and we made money off of it and
we put it out.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:04.040
I can say this now, I’m still selling DVDs.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:12.600
I 
was still doing screenings of the film.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:23.800
That’s how I was able to make a living is
by screening these films, making DVDs, going

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:35.670
online (just don’t depend on Youtube or
Apple).

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:40.250
You have to go out and screen your film.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:42.770
We have to make money off of our films.

